  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will build overhead tonight. The combination of\nwinds trending calm along with a dry airmass aloft (-1 to -2 \nSTDEV) will allow for ideal radiational cooling conditions. Low\ntemperatures by daybreak Tuesday should fall into the 40s for \nmost areas, except mid to upper 30s across portions of the \nsouthern Adirondacks/southern VT and eastern Catskills. Some \npatchy frost is expected in the more sheltered areas within the \nSW Adirondacks and possibly eastern Catskills. However, coverage\npotential for frost looks isolated at this time, therefore no \nfrost advisory was issued. Nevertheless, those with sensitive \nplants/vegetation may want to take precautions in these areas.\nSome patchy valley fog could also form late tonight.\n\nHigh pressure slides off to the east by tomorrow allowing for a\nsouth to southwesterly return flow to resume. It will remain\nfall-like Tuesday before temperatures rebound by midweek back\ninto the upper 70s to around 80 in the valleys and lower to\nmid-70s in the higher elevations. These temperatures will\ncontinue through the end of the week. A front will approach the\narea Tuesday night into early Wednesday but looks to wash out.\nPending how quickly it washes out, there could be a few showers\nmainly north and west of Albany but overall confidence on this \nis low. As a result, we kept a dry forecast at this time.\n\nAnother cold front will approach the area later Wednesday into\nThursday. In addition, precipitable water values will increase \nto at or above 1.50 inches (+2 to +3 STDEV). Enough forcing \nalong the front could allow for some showers and thunderstorms \nwith Thursday the most likely day for activity at this time. \nThe high precipitable water values could lead to some heavy \ndownpours. High pressure should shift the front south enough for\ndry weather on Friday before it lifts back northward with the \npotential for additional rain and thunderstorms next weekend as \nanother system approaches from the west.",
